<article class="page-prose"><section id="programme" class="prose-section"><h2>A working research agenda</h2><p>Galapagensis investigates the dispersed documentary history, memory and knowledge of the Galápagos Islands. Its research lines connect evidence held across institutions, collections, communities and countries.</p><p>This page presents the twelve lines of Research Agenda v0.1 (3 September 2026). Their horizons describe research intentions and priorities; they do not imply that every investigation or output is already underway. The agenda remains open to revision.</p></section><section id="line-1" class="prose-section"><h2>The Distributed Galápagos Archive</h2><p><strong>Active</strong></p><p>Where are the documentary traces of Galápagos history now held, and what becomes visible when they are treated as parts of one dispersed corpus rather than as isolated institutional holdings?</p><p>Archives, libraries, museums, scientific collections, government records, expedition papers, newspapers, maps, photographs, personal papers, family collections, digitized material and private holdings in Ecuador and worldwide.</p><p>Possible investigations include  international repository and collection mapping; dispersed records of specific expeditions; reconstruction of collections split across institutions; tracing documents, specimens and photographs back to common historical events.</p><p><strong>Possible outputs:</strong> Repository directory, collection map, Research Dossiers, datasets, Stories, annotated bibliography, articles.</p></section><section id="line-2" class="prose-section"><h2>People Missing from the Record</h2><p><strong>Active / Coming next</strong></p><p>Whose lives and work disappear when Galápagos history is reconstructed mainly through institutional archives, scientific publications and famous visitors?</p><p>Women, workers, settlers, guides, sailors, fishers, farmers, teachers, families, local assistants, technicians, migrants and other people whose documentary presence is partial or uneven.</p><p>Possible investigations include  women in science, settlement and community life; workers behind scientific expeditions; unidentified people in photographs; family histories; technical and support staff omitted from institutional narratives.</p><p><strong>Possible outputs:</strong> Women pathway, biographies, Dossiers, oral histories, Stories, family-history compilations, exhibitions, books.</p></section><section id="line-3" class="prose-section"><h2>Voices and Living Memory</h2><p><strong>Coming next</strong></p><p>What survives in memory, testimony and local knowledge that formal archives do not preserve?</p><p>Oral histories, family narratives, recollections, place-based memory, local terminology, remembered events, community knowledge and private photographic interpretation.</p><p>Possible investigations include  remembered settlements and vanished places; family histories; identification of people and locations in photographs; historical environmental memory; local accounts of institutional or community change.</p><p><strong>Possible outputs:</strong> Oral-history collection, transcripts, Stories, Dossiers, audio features, Para llevar resources.</p></section><section id="line-4" class="prose-section"><h2>Galápagos in Motion</h2><p><strong>Active</strong></p><p>How have movements of people, vessels, goods, knowledge and documents shaped Galápagos history?</p><p>Navigation, voyages, migration, settlement, shipping, expedition routes, provisioning, ports, travel networks and documentary circulation.</p><p>Possible investigations include  maritime routes; migration networks; movement between islands; ships as historical actors; circulation of specimens, photographs and papers.</p><p><strong>Possible outputs:</strong> Maps, route reconstructions, vessel pages, Stories, Dossiers, datasets, timelines.</p></section><section id="line-5" class="prose-section"><h2>Science in the Islands</h2><p><strong>Active</strong></p><p>How was scientific knowledge about Galápagos actually produced, by whom, through which expeditions, institutions, materials and practices?</p><p>Fieldwork, expeditions, collecting, specimens, instruments, laboratories, research stations, assistants, scientific correspondence, taxonomic work and scientific networks.</p><p>Possible investigations include  dispersed expedition archives; specimen histories; scientific assistants and overlooked participants; photographic documentation of fieldwork; changing collecting practices; institutional scientific networks.</p><p><strong>Possible outputs:</strong> Dossiers, expedition reconstructions, specimen histories, Stories, articles, books, datasets.</p></section><section id="line-6" class="prose-section"><h2>Material Galápagos</h2><p><strong>Coming next</strong></p><p>What can buildings, ruins, roads, objects, archaeological sites and landscapes reveal that written records cannot?</p><p>Archaeology, architecture, infrastructure, artifacts, abandoned structures, military remains, penal remains, historical landscapes and material culture.</p><p>Possible investigations include  surviving penal structures; military remains; early settlement infrastructure; archaeological collections held off-island; historical routes and landscapes; unidentified artifacts.</p><p><strong>Possible outputs:</strong> Site records, maps, photographic documentation, Dossiers, Stories, archaeological inventories.</p></section><section id="line-7" class="prose-section"><h2>Lives with the Living World</h2><p><strong>Coming next</strong></p><p>How have people used, altered, studied, depended upon and lived alongside Galápagos species and ecosystems?</p><p>Fishing, whaling, sealing, hunting, collecting, agriculture, livestock, introduced species, food, resource use, ecological transformation and subsistence.</p><p>Possible investigations include  fisheries history; whaling and sealing; introduced domestic animals; agriculture and rural life; changing uses of marine and terrestrial species; historical ecological observations.</p><p><strong>Possible outputs:</strong> Environmental histories, datasets, Stories, maps, Dossiers, teaching resources.</p></section><section id="line-8" class="prose-section"><h2>Governance, Confinement and Power</h2><p><strong>Coming next</strong></p><p>How have states, institutions and coercive systems governed, occupied, regulated and controlled Galápagos?</p><p>Sovereignty, administration, law, penal colonies, military presence, territorial claims, policing, public policy and institutional authority.</p><p>Possible investigations include  penal colonies; Baltra and wartime occupation; annexation and sovereignty; administrative reforms; conflicts over jurisdiction and land; institutional control of mobility and resources.</p><p><strong>Possible outputs:</strong> Dossiers, timelines, maps, document editions, Stories, articles.</p></section><section id="line-9" class="prose-section"><h2>Making Galápagos Visible</h2><p><strong>Active / Coming next</strong></p><p>How has Galápagos been represented, imagined and made visible through images, maps, publishing and cultural production?</p><p>Photography, film, cartography, journalism, travel writing, illustration, art, publishing, postcards and visual culture.</p><p>Possible investigations include  expedition photography; changing visual tropes; historical maps and place names; journalism and publicity; artistic representations; tourism imagery.</p><p><strong>Possible outputs:</strong> Visual essays, exhibitions, image collections, Stories, books, map projects.</p></section><section id="line-10" class="prose-section"><h2>Conservation as History</h2><p><strong>Active / Longer-term expansion</strong></p><p>How did conservation become one of the dominant ways of understanding and governing Galápagos?</p><p>Conservation institutions, policy, protected areas, invasive species control, restoration, environmental education, scientific advocacy, conflicts and changing environmental ideas.</p><p>Possible investigations include  origins of conservation institutions; competing conservation models; conflicts between conservation and local livelihoods; environmental education; changing narratives of degradation and restoration.</p><p><strong>Possible outputs:</strong> Institutional histories, Dossiers, Stories, document collections, articles.</p></section><section id="line-11" class="prose-section"><h2>Everyday Island Life</h2><p><strong>Longer research horizon</strong></p><p>What did ordinary life in Galápagos look, sound and feel like beyond expeditions, institutions and major historical events?</p><p>Education, work, housing, health, food, family life, leisure, religion, transport, domestic practices, community organizations and local economies.</p><p>Possible investigations include  schools and teachers; foodways; rural and domestic life; health and medicine; local commerce; community clubs and associations; childhood and family life.</p><p><strong>Possible outputs:</strong> Oral histories, Stories, thematic collections, photo essays, books, Para llevar.</p></section><section id="line-12" class="prose-section"><h2>How Galápagos History Was Made</h2><p><strong>Active</strong></p><p>How did particular stories about Galápagos become accepted as history, and what happens when their documentary lineages are reconstructed?</p><p>Historiography, myths, famous anecdotes, repeated errors, documentary mediation, lost sources, historical silences and changing interpretations.</p><p>Possible investigations include  genealogy of the Patrick Watkins story; authorship and provenance problems; repeated historical claims without surviving source bases; transformation of documentary evidence into cultural myth; institutional histories of forgetting and rediscovery.</p><p><strong>Possible outputs:</strong> Research Dossiers, “How do we know?” features, historiographical essays, articles, books, teaching resources.</p></section><p><a href="/research/georgina-lloyd-taylor/">Read the Georgina Lloyd Taylor research dossier</a></p><section id="development" class="prose-section"><h2>Research, outputs and support</h2><p>Research may produce source records, documented relationships, biographies, maps, datasets, Research Dossiers, Stories, critical editions, articles and books. Outputs are published progressively, with evidence and unresolved questions identified.</p><p>The working agenda also asks whether environmental history, representation, conservation and everyday life should remain separate sustained lines or become cross-cutting thematic areas. It leaves open whether knowledge circulation and the movement of collections require a distinct line.</p><p>Archival access, reproduction and digitization, travel, transcription, translation, research assistance and community or institutional collaboration can expand this work. Funding should accelerate genuine research intentions rather than determine the subjects the programme claims to care about.</p><p><a href="mailto:contact@galapagensis.net">contact@galapagensis.net</a></p></section></article></div></section>
